  "title": "Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income): bounded route itemizes line 7 unemployment from the closed Form 1099-G box-1 family and line 21 student loan interest from the closed Form 1098-E box-1 family; required when either closed subtotal is strictly greater than zero; completeness value-checks Alternative A Part I absences, repayment-not-present, and residual Form 1099-G payment-class absences (unchanged from v1 -- Track 0 T0-6/T0-7 settlement adds no new completeness.required_answers entries for Part II). Not a general Schedule 1 claim; Part II adjustments other than line 21 remain out of scope. Track 0 T0-7 settlement (docs/phases/engine-breadth/milestones/f1098e-student-loan-interest-agi.md): same rule identity succeeding forward on the existing attachment-rule.v4 grammar (requirement.subtotals and itemizations are already arrays; no new schema shape needed -- the milestone's pre-filed attachment-rule.v9 ledger claim is not exercised, see that rule file's own notes for the reconciliation). Track 4b repair (same milestone document, Track 4b): Track 4 originally tied the line-21 itemization's row_set out to tax.us.2025.sli-worksheet.line1-total-interest-paid-subtotal while that symbol was the $2,500-CAPPED worksheet-line-1 figure, which falsely blocked any filer whose true box-1 interest exceeded $2,500 with ITEMIZATION_TIE_OUT_VIOLATION even though line 21 computed correctly. Track 4b fixed this upstream, in rule.sli-worksheet-line1-subtotal.json, not here: that sibling rule now publishes the family's RAW, uncapped closure-authorized sum (the true ADR-0016 authority object, satisfying audit_collect_authority exactly), and the worksheet's own $2,500 cap moved into rule.sli-worksheet.json's own line-1 reads. This file needed no change -- the itemization row_set below still ties out to the same symbol name, tax.us.2025.sli-worksheet.line1-total-interest-paid-subtotal, but that symbol's value is now the raw sum, so it equals the itemization's own raw row-level member sum by construction for every filer, cap-bound or not. Proven, not assumed: tests/test_schedule1_line26_track4.py::test_over_cap_filer_itemization_no_longer_false_blocks exercises the over-cap case and confirms the attachment now publishes.",
  "version": "v2"
